{{smaller block|Rending her yeolow locks, like wyrie gold
About her shoulders careleslie downe ''trailing.''
{{right|''Spenser'', Ruins of Time, l. 11.}}}}

'''2.''' To grow loosely and without self-support to a considerable length along the ground or over bushes, rocks, or other low objects; recline or droop and as it were drag upon the ground, as a branch. See ''trailing plant'', below.—'''3.''' To move with a slow sweeping motion.

{{smaller block|And through the momentary gloom
Of shadows o'er the landscape ''trailing.''
{{right|''Longfellow'', Golden Legend, iv.}}}}

'''4.''' To loiter or creep along as a straggler or a person who is nearly tired out; walk or make one's way idly or lazily.

{{smaller block|He ''trails'' along the streets.
''Character of a Town-Gallant'' (1675), p. 5. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}

{{smaller block|We ''trailed'' wearily along the level road.
{{right|''The Century'', XXIII. 654.}}}}

'''5†.''' To reach or extend in a straggling way.

{{smaller block|Cape Roxo is a low Cape and ''trayling'' to the sea-ward.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 615.}}}}

'''6.''' To fish with or from a trailer: as, to ''trail''

