'''train'''<sup>1</sup> (trān), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''traine'', ''trayne''; < ME. ''trainen'', ''traynen'', < OF. ''trainer'', ''trahiner'', F. ''traîner'' = Pr. ''trainar'' = Sp. ''trajinar'' = It. ''trainare'', draw, entice, trail along, < ML. ''trahinare'', drag along, trail, < L. ''trahere'', draw: see ''tract''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''trail''<sup>1</sup>, from the same source. Hence ''train''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' For the sense 'educate,' from the lit. sense 'draw,' cf. ''educate'', ult. < L. ''educare'', draw out.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To draw or drag along; trail.

{{smaller block|So he hath hir ''trayned'' and drawen that the lady myght no lenger crye ne brayen. ''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 299.}}

{{smaller block|Not distant far with heavy pace the foe<br>Approaching gross and huge; in hollow cube<br>''Training'' his devilish enginery.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., vi. 553.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|What pitie is it that any . . . man shulde . . . be ''trayned'' . . . in to this lothesome dungeon [idleness].
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, i. 26.}}}}

{{smaller block|We did ''train'' him on,
And, his corruption being ta'en from us,
We, as the spring of all, shall pay for all.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., v. 2. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|With pretext of doing him an unwonted honour in the senate, he ''trains'' him from his guards.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sejanus, Arg.}}}}

{{smaller block|Martius Galeotti, who, by his impostures and specious falsehoods, has ''trained'' me hither into the power of my mortal enemy.
{{right|''Scott'', Quentin Durward, xxviii.}}}}

'''3.''' To bring into some desired course or state by means of some process of instruction and exercise. {{smaller|(''a'') To educate; instruct; rear; bring up: often with ''up''.}}

{{smaller block|So was she ''trayned up'' from time to time
In all chaste vertue and true bounti-hed.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. vi. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Train up'' a child in the way he should go, and when [even when, R. V.] he is old he will not depart from it.
{{right|Prov. xxii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|You have ''trained'' me like a peasant.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 1. 71.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') To make proficient or efficient, as in some art or profession, by instruction, exercise, or discipline; make proficient by instruction or drill: as, to ''train'' nurses; to ''train'' soldiers.}}

{{smaller block|And when Abram heard that his brother was taken captive, he armed his ''trained'' servants.
{{right|Gen. xiv. 14.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Trained'' in camps, he knew the art
To win the soldier's hardy heart.
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, iii. 4.}}}}

{{smaller|(''c'') To tame or render docile; exercise in the performance of certain tasks or tricks: as, to ''train'' dogs or monkeys.}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Animals can be ''trained'' by man, but they cannot ''train'' themselves. They can be taught some accomplishments, formed to some new habits; but where man has not done this for them they remain uneducated.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 33.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''d'') To fit by proper exercise and regimen for the performance of some feat; render capable of enduring the strain incident to a contest of any kind, by a course of suitable exercise, regimen, etc.; put in suitable condition, as for a race, by preparatory exercise, etc.: as, to ''train'' a boat's crew for a race. (''e'') To give proper or some particular shape or direction to by systematic manipulation or extension; specifically, in ''gardening'', to extend the branches of, as on a wall, espalier, etc.}}

{{smaller block|Tell her, when I'm gone, to ''train'' the rose-bush that I set
About the parlour-window.
{{right|''Tennyson'', May Queen, New-Year's Eve.}}
Why will she ''train'' that winter curl
In such a spring-like way?
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', My Aunt.}}}}

'''4.''' To bring to bear; direct or aim carefully: as, to ''train'' a gun upon a vessel or a fort.

{{smaller block|Again and again we set up the camera, and ''trained'' it upon a part of the picturesque throng.
{{right|''G. Kennan'', The Century, XXXVIII. 73.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''To train a scent'''†, in ''hunting'', same as ''to carry a scent.'' See phrase under ''scent.''}}

{{smaller block|I ha' seene one Sheepe worry a dozen Foxes,
By Moon-shine, in a morning before day,
They hunt, ''trayne-sents'' with Oxen, and plow with Dogges.
{{right|''Brome'', The Antipodes, i. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''To train fine.''' See ''fine''<sup>2</sup>. ='''Syn. 3.''' To school, habituate, inure. See ''instruction.''}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1'''†. To be attracted or lured.

{{smaller block|The highest soaring Hauke ''traineth'' to ye lure.
{{right|''Lyly'', Euphues, Anat. of Wit, p. 35.}}}}

'''2.''' To exercise; impart proficiency by practice and use; drill; discipline.

readings.c3s3:
¶

{{smaller block|Nature ''trains'' while she teaches; she disciplines the powers while she imparts information to the intellect.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, Int., p. 11.}}}}

'''3.''' To fit one's self for the performance of some feat by preparatory regimen and exercise.

{{smaller block|So he resolved at once to ''train'',<br>And walked and walked with all his main.
{{right|''W. S. Gilbert'', Perils of Invisibility.}}}}

'''4.''' To be under training, as a recruit for the army; be drilled for military service.—'''5.''' To travel by train or by rail: sometimes with an indefinite ''it.'' [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|From Aberdeen to Edinburgh we ''trained it'' by easy stages.
{{right|''Harper's Mag.'', LXXVII. 954.}}}}

'''6.''' To consort with; be on familiar terms with: as, I don't ''train'' with that crowd. Compare def. 4. [Slang.]—'''7.''' To romp; carry on. [Colloq. and vulgar, U. S.]—{{smaller|'''To train off''', to go off obliquely: said of the flight of a shot.}}

